          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combination Wrenches</h3>
          <p>
            Tekton's standard chrome series combination wrenches are the brand's strongest value proposition. Chrome vanadium construction, tight jaw tolerances, and comprehensive SAE/metric coverage make them the go-to choice for most mechanics. They outperform GearWrench and Pittsburgh in fit and finish at comparable prices, and they close the gap on Snap-on and Craftsman USA for a fraction of the cost. The one area where Snap-on maintains a genuine lead is the Flank Drive Plus geometry that prevents rounding on damaged fasteners — a feature that matters in professional shop environments but is rarely decisive for home use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Socket Sets</h3>
          <p>
            Tekton's chrome vanadium and chrome moly socket lines deliver solid broach geometry and reliable fit. They outclass Pittsburgh and Husky in socket-to-fastener fit, which directly affects how much damage is done to fastener flats over repeated use. Snap-on and SK sockets have finer tolerances and more refined finishes, but at price multiples that aren't justified for anything outside a professional automotive shop billing labor at $150+ per hour. For DIY and light professional use, Tekton sockets are the correct choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Torque Wrenches</h3>
          <p>
            Tekton's click-type torque wrenches deliver verified accuracy within ±4% through the working range — within the specification required for critical fasteners. CDI (a professional-grade torque wrench brand) offers marginally better accuracy at significantly higher prices. Snap-on's electronic torque wrenches are the precision standard for specialty applications. For lug nuts, cylinder heads, and suspension fasteners in a home or light professional setting, Tekton's click-type delivers everything you need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hex Keys</h3>
          <p>
            The S2 steel ball-end hex key set is arguably Tekton's most compelling single product. S2 steel is genuinely superior to the chrome vanadium or plain carbon steel used by most competitors at this price point. Bondhus standard hex keys are a respected alternative at similar pricing, but their standard chrome vanadium tips show more wear under comparable testing. The European specialty brands — Wera ball-end and PB Swiss — produce measurably better tip geometry and finish, but at two to three times the price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Screwdrivers</h3>
          <p>
            Tekton's acetate handle screwdrivers are a strong performer for general shop use, beating Pittsburgh and Husky in tip hardness and handle ergonomics. Where they fall short is against Wera's Lasertip technology, which uses a micro-textured tip surface to grip worn Phillips cam recesses with dramatically better cam-out resistance. For a set that will handle new fasteners in clean condition, Tekton delivers. For a shop that frequently deals with rusted or previously stripped fasteners, Wera is worth the premium.
          </p>

