          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Build a Complete Tekton Toolkit on a Budget
          </h2>

          <p>
            Building a complete hand tool kit doesn't require spending thousands at once. The right approach is to start with high-frequency tools and add specialty items as actual jobs demand them. Here's the sequence that gives you the best capability per dollar spent at each stage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Start with the Combination Wrench Set</h3>
          <p>
            The combination wrench set is the most versatile hand tool in any shop. It handles bolt heads and nuts across every system on a vehicle or in a home — from engine work to furniture assembly to plumbing. The Tekton 30-piece set covers both SAE and metric in one purchase, eliminating the common frustration of hunting for the "other" set. Budget $80-120 for the full set and keep it accessible at all times. This single purchase handles roughly 60% of all fastener work you'll ever encounter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Add a Socket and Ratchet Set</h3>
          <p>
            The socket and ratchet combination handles recessed fasteners and provides torque advantages that combination wrenches can't match. The 40-piece Tekton set with both 1/4 and 3/8-inch drive gives you coverage from small electronics and engine sensor work up through medium suspension fasteners. Add the 3-piece ratchet set separately if you want all three drive sizes. Budget $60-100 for the socket set and $50-70 for the ratchets. Combined with the wrench set, you now have the tools to handle 85% of all mechanical work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Invest in a Torque Wrench Before You Need One</h3>
          <p>
            The torque wrench is the most commonly skipped tool purchase — until the first blown head gasket or wheel that comes off because lug nuts were uneven. Don't wait for a catastrophic failure to justify this purchase. The Tekton 1/2-inch click torque wrench covers lug nuts (typically 80-100 ft-lbs), cylinder head bolts (varies by engine but commonly 50-80 ft-lbs), and suspension fasteners (60-100+ ft-lbs). Budget $45-55. This is one of the highest-value purchases in terms of damage prevented per dollar spent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Get the Hex Key Set for Modern Fasteners</h3>
          <p>
            Modern vehicles, bicycles, furniture, and appliances are held together with Allen bolts — socket head cap screws that require hex keys. The proliferation of Allen fasteners in automotive work over the past two decades means no toolkit is complete without a quality hex key set. The Tekton S2 ball-end set gives you the off-axis engagement capability needed for the tight spaces where Allen bolts are increasingly used. Budget $25-35 for the 30-piece set.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Add Specialty Tools as Jobs Demand</h3>
          <p>
            After the four core purchases above, resist the urge to buy anticipatory tools you may never use. Instead, add specialty items when a specific job requires them. The screwdriver set is the next logical addition for any shop dealing with electrical components, trim panels, or household appliances. The breaker bar earns its place the first time you encounter a truly seized suspension fastener. A 1/2-inch drive socket set for larger fasteners follows once you're tackling wheel work or major mechanical jobs. This demand-driven approach keeps costs controlled and ensures every tool you own gets used.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Warranty -->
        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Tekton's Lifetime Warranty: What It Actually Covers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton's lifetime hassle-free warranty covers all hand tools for life against manufacturing defects and normal use failures. The scope is broad: if a wrench snaps under normal torque application, if a ratchet mechanism fails, if a socket cracks under use — Tekton replaces it. No receipt is required, no purchase documentation, no registration process. The warranty is tied to the product, not the buyer, which means it covers second-hand Tekton tools purchased used.
          </p>

          <p>
            We tested the warranty process directly. A ratchet mechanism in our 3-piece set began exhibiting intermittent slipping under load after approximately four months of daily testing — a failure mode that shouldn't occur in a quality ratchet. We contacted Tekton through their website warranty portal, submitted a brief description and a photo of the tool. The response came within 24 hours: a replacement unit was approved and shipped the same day, before the defective ratchet was returned. The return shipping label was included in the warranty confirmation email. Total time from initial contact to replacement in hand: four days. That process is operationally equivalent to Snap-on's warranty service through a dealer, at a fraction of the initial tool cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the warranty does not cover is deliberate abuse — using a hand tool as a hammer, extending handles with cheater pipes on tools not rated for that use, or attempting repairs that require specialty tools you're using a hand tool to substitute for. This is a reasonable boundary, and Tekton applies it consistently. The warranty also doesn't cover wear items like pouch fabric or case hinges, which are not structural tool components. For structural tool failures under normal use, the warranty is genuine, responsive, and worth factoring into your purchasing decision. A Tekton tool that fails gets replaced for free; the same failure in a Pittsburgh tool from Harbor Freight typically ends in a store return battle.
          </p>

