        <!-- SECTION 6: Tekton vs Competitors -->
        <div id="tekton-vs-competitors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Tekton vs. the Competition
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tekton vs. Snap-on</h3>
          <p>
            Snap-on is the gold standard of professional hand tools, and the comparison is instructive rather than competitive. Snap-on's advantages are real and specific: the Flank Drive Plus wrench geometry genuinely reduces fastener rounding on damaged hardware; the 120-tooth ratchet mechanism provides a 3-degree arc swing versus Tekton's 5-degree; the chrome finish quality and surface tolerances are measurably finer. These differences matter most in a professional flat-rate shop environment where the tool you're using affects how fast you can diagnose and complete jobs, directly impacting your income.
          </p>
          <p>
            For everyone else — the home mechanic, the weekend restorer, the DIY homeowner — Snap-on's advantages are real but not decisive. The Tekton combination wrench set we tested would not have produced a different outcome on any of the jobs we threw at it than the Snap-on equivalent. The fasteners moved. The tools held. The money saved — $400-500 on a wrench set alone — is real and immediate. Tekton is the correct choice for anyone who cannot justify Snap-on pricing with professional income.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tekton vs. GearWrench</h3>
          <p>
            GearWrench competes directly with Tekton at similar price points and is the most frequently cited alternative. GearWrench's combination wrenches feature their XL Beam ratcheting box-end design — a genuine functionality advantage for enclosed spaces. Tekton's standard box-end requires repositioning on each stroke; the GearWrench ratcheting design doesn't. For fasteners in tight spaces where the box end is being used, GearWrench wins that specific comparison. For everything else — open-end work, overall set quality, warranty service responsiveness — Tekton is comparable or slightly better. The right call is to consider whether you're buying primarily for ratcheting wrench functionality (GearWrench) or for overall set quality at the best price (Tekton).
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tekton vs. Pittsburgh / Harbor Freight</h3>
          <p>
            This is the most important comparison for budget-conscious buyers, because Pittsburgh is widely available and genuinely cheap. After testing Pittsburgh tools against Tekton equivalents, the differences are measurable and meaningful. Pittsburgh socket broach geometry is looser — we documented this with calipers and observed it in fastener flat wear after repeated use. Pittsburgh wrench jaw tolerances are wider, which increases the likelihood of rounding on tight fasteners. The Pittsburgh ratchet mechanisms we tested showed rougher engagement and less consistent click-feel than Tekton equivalents. None of this means Pittsburgh tools are non-functional. They work. But the gap in real-world performance is noticeable from the first day of use, and the gap in durability compounds over months of use. Tekton is worth the modest price premium over Pittsburgh for anyone who expects their tools to last.
          </p>
        </div>

